1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,757
This is RDT, the Remote Debugger Tool.

2
00:00:04,057 --> 00:00:14,596
It reads, watches and changes the variables and memory of running firmware, over the link your product already has, using each MCU’s ELF file.

3
00:00:14,896 --> 00:00:18,032
Let’s watch someone use it, against the simulator.

4
00:00:20,132 --> 00:00:28,452
This is RDT, the Remote Debugger Tool, open on the example workspace, with the simulator running on this PC.

5
00:00:28,902 --> 00:00:33,489
Across the top are the menus: File, Target, Watch, View and Help.

6
00:00:35,839 --> 00:00:41,023
The toolbar starts with the panel buttons: Watch, Scope, Memory and Console.

7
00:00:41,514 --> 00:00:47,445
Then the link: the transport, and the IP address and port, or the COM port and baud rate.

8
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:53,655
The gear opens the SCPI profile, and the connect button shows the state of the link.

9
00:00:54,105 --> 00:01:00,015
Then RUN and STOP for polling, the polling interval, Poll now, and Log CSV.

10
00:01:00,465 --> 00:01:09,404
On the left is the Target panel, with a card for each MCU. In the middle are the watch tabs, and along the bottom, the status bar.

11
00:01:09,854 --> 00:01:15,294
To start from scratch, there’s File, New workspace, or File, Open. I’ll keep this one.

12
00:01:15,897 --> 00:01:23,385
The link can be changed while disconnected. Serial shows the COM port and baud rate. I’ll go back to TCP.

13
00:01:25,571 --> 00:01:31,950
The gear opens the SCPI profile: the read and write commands your firmware understands.

14
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:38,651
The preview shows each command exactly as it will be sent, with the address as eight hex digits.

15
00:01:39,101 --> 00:01:45,757
Load from file fills the fields from a profile file, and Test read tries the profile once connected.

16
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:49,919
Target, MCUs and ELF files lists every MCU.

17
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:57,456
Each has an id, a name, a short name and colour for its tag, its ELF file, and its own timeout.

18
00:01:59,837 --> 00:02:02,312
A double-click on a colour picks another one.

19
00:02:02,763 --> 00:02:07,883
Memory ranges come from a memory map file, with File, Import, Memory map.

20
00:02:09,988 --> 00:02:13,742
I pick the file, and add its ranges to the ones I have.

21
00:02:14,982 --> 00:02:21,297
Each card’s Memory ranges list shows what RDT may read and write on that MCU.

22
00:02:21,747 --> 00:02:26,867
Known commands for the console come in the same way, with File, Import, Commands.

23
00:02:30,684 --> 00:02:33,308
Now I connect, with the button or F5.

24
00:02:33,758 --> 00:02:40,670
It turns green. Connected means the socket is open; RDT sends nothing to test the device.

25
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:47,904
Each card checks its ELF file against the firmware. The main MCU matches, but motorctl doesn’t.

26
00:02:48,355 --> 00:02:53,987
The red bar says so, and every read and write to motorctl is refused until it’s fixed.

27
00:02:54,437 --> 00:02:59,962
Load ELF opens the MCU list. I select motorctl’s ELF, and press Browse.

28
00:03:00,412 --> 00:03:04,082
I pick the ELF that matches the firmware, and press OK.

29
00:03:05,304 --> 00:03:08,845
Both cards are green now, and the red bar is gone.

30
00:03:10,197 --> 00:03:16,746
A right-click on a card checks its ELF again, edits the MCUs, or opens it in the memory view.

31
00:03:17,196 --> 00:03:24,748
If a secondary MCU stops answering, only its card turns silent. The other MCUs keep working.

32
00:03:25,198 --> 00:03:28,868
When it answers again, RDT picks it up by itself.

33
00:03:29,318 --> 00:03:37,531
If the whole board stops answering, the button turns red: link down. Writes are refused, and the last values stay in grey.

34
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:41,032
When the replies come back, it goes green again.

35
00:03:42,482 --> 00:03:50,098
The Motor tab is live. Each row starts with its MCU’s tag, and a value that just changed flashes yellow.

36
00:03:50,548 --> 00:03:53,322
Hovering over a name shows the full expression.

37
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:59,254
To add variables, I open the main card’s Variables, and type in the search box.

38
00:03:59,705 --> 00:04:02,862
A double-click adds a variable to the tab in front.

39
00:04:03,312 --> 00:04:06,533
Or I select several, and press Add to watch list.

40
00:04:10,454 --> 00:04:13,291
Variables can also be dragged onto a tab.

41
00:04:15,275 --> 00:04:21,035
Watch, Add expression, or Control E, takes any expression, such as one cell of an array.

42
00:04:21,486 --> 00:04:28,462
Structures and arrays open to every field and cell. A closed one isn’t read, which keeps the link free.

43
00:04:29,134 --> 00:04:32,804
Enums show their names, and char arrays show as text.

44
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:39,936
A right-click on a field can give it its own row.

45
00:04:40,386 --> 00:04:47,767
Hovering over a row shows two small buttons: a wave to chart it, and a cross to remove it. I’ll remove this one.

46
00:04:48,217 --> 00:04:52,313
Each tab is its own watch list. The plus button makes a new one.

47
00:04:52,764 --> 00:04:54,854
A double-click on a tab renames it.

48
00:04:56,172 --> 00:04:58,604
Tabs can be dragged into a new order.

49
00:05:00,739 --> 00:05:06,883
Also show in tab puts the same item in another tab. It stays one item, read once.

50
00:05:09,846 --> 00:05:16,289
Control Page Down moves to the next tab, and every tab keeps polling, not only the one in front.

51
00:05:16,739 --> 00:05:21,816
A right-click on an item sets its format: decimal, hex, binary or char.

52
00:05:22,266 --> 00:05:29,285
Settings holds the fixed-point format, scale, offset and unit, the item’s polling, and its write safety.

53
00:05:33,434 --> 00:05:37,658
This one shows milliamps as amps, with a scale of one thousandth.

54
00:05:38,955 --> 00:05:44,267
To change a value, I double-click it, type the new value in its units, and press Enter.

55
00:05:44,718 --> 00:05:51,118
This item asks before writing, so I confirm. RDT writes it, and reads it back to check.

56
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:56,752
A value outside the item’s limits is refused, with the reason in the red bar.

57
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:05,511
Motor mode is read-only, so it’s grey. Watch, All items read-only, protects every item at once.

58
00:06:09,130 --> 00:06:11,967
STOP, or F7, pauses all polling.

59
00:06:12,418 --> 00:06:15,468
Poll now, or F6, reads everything once.

60
00:06:15,918 --> 00:06:19,780
The box beside RUN sets the global polling interval.

61
00:06:20,230 --> 00:06:26,225
Each item can follow it, have its own interval, or be read only by hand, with Read now.

62
00:06:26,675 --> 00:06:32,691
RUN starts polling again. The Rate column shows how often each item is really read.

63
00:06:33,141 --> 00:06:34,933
The Scope button shows the chart.

64
00:06:35,383 --> 00:06:39,714
Add to chart puts a value on it, drawn in its MCU’s colour.

65
00:06:41,901 --> 00:06:46,936
Or I select a row and press Control G, or drag rows onto the chart.

66
00:06:59,967 --> 00:07:02,292
Window sets how much time is shown.

67
00:07:03,603 --> 00:07:09,107
Space pauses the view while the data keeps coming. The wheel zooms, and dragging pans.

68
00:07:15,627 --> 00:07:19,211
Fit Y rescales, and Run follows the data again.

69
00:07:20,955 --> 00:07:25,563
Cursors show each signal’s value at two points, and the time between them.

70
00:07:29,649 --> 00:07:35,708
A right-click on a signal moves it to another Y axis, changes its colour, or removes it.

71
00:07:41,757 --> 00:07:47,346
Control T adds minimum, maximum, mean and sample columns to the watch tree.

72
00:07:48,689 --> 00:07:54,833
Log CSV, or Control L, writes every value to a file you can open in Excel.

73
00:07:57,172 --> 00:07:59,263
Pressing it again stops the log.

74
00:08:01,157 --> 00:08:06,767
The Memory button shows raw memory. I type a variable name and a length, and press Read.

75
00:08:10,293 --> 00:08:13,215
Refresh reads it again at the polling interval.

76
00:08:13,666 --> 00:08:18,210
A right-click changes how cells show, such as signed shorts, in decimal.

77
00:08:23,283 --> 00:08:27,443
Or as floats, where the motor’s speed and target are easy to read.

78
00:08:27,893 --> 00:08:33,397
A double-click on a cell writes a new value. This one is the target, so it asks first.

79
00:08:34,747 --> 00:08:40,294
The Console button opens a terminal to the firmware. I type a query and press Enter.

80
00:08:40,745 --> 00:08:46,356
A failed command gets no reply at all. The error query reads why from the firmware.

81
00:08:46,806 --> 00:08:52,524
As I type, matching commands appear, with the best one in grey. The right arrow takes it.

82
00:08:52,974 --> 00:08:54,851
Up walks back through the history.

83
00:08:55,302 --> 00:09:01,083
Commands lists the known commands I imported. A double-click puts one on the prompt line.

84
00:09:01,533 --> 00:09:04,243
History lists what I sent, to send again.

85
00:09:08,819 --> 00:09:14,579
Control F searches the transcript, and Control plus and minus change the text size.

86
00:09:15,913 --> 00:09:21,481
Timestamps and Wrap change the view, and Log to file saves the session to a text file.

87
00:09:22,921 --> 00:09:27,743
A right-click sends a break on a COM port, or turns a selection into hex.

88
00:09:28,193 --> 00:09:32,310
The panel buttons, or Control 1 to 4, show and hide their panels.

89
00:09:37,126 --> 00:09:41,990
Drag a panel by its title to dock it somewhere else, stack it, or float it.

90
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:55,511
View, Target hides the MCU cards. Save layout keeps the arrangement, and Reset layout puts every panel back.

91
00:10:03,108 --> 00:10:06,948
View, Density switches between compact and comfortable rows.

92
00:10:21,453 --> 00:10:25,826
View, Theme changes the look. Here’s Islands, with rounded panels.

93
00:10:30,854 --> 00:10:32,944
Instrument, made for scope work.

94
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:41,271
Light pro.

95
00:10:49,830 --> 00:10:51,643
And back to Studio dark.

96
00:10:58,318 --> 00:11:04,505
A star in the title means unsaved changes. Control S saves the workspace and its files.

97
00:11:04,967 --> 00:11:09,917
File, Export saves one part at a time, such as the watch list, to share it.

98
00:11:16,827 --> 00:11:23,697
File, Import brings it into another workspace, adding to its tabs or replacing them. Here I’ll cancel.

99
00:11:32,016 --> 00:11:36,794
Interface settings, with your theme and console setup, export the same way.

100
00:11:43,570 --> 00:11:49,160
Help has the user manual, the release notes, the update check, and the keyboard shortcuts.

101
00:11:57,595 --> 00:11:59,622
F1 lists every shortcut.

102
00:12:00,475 --> 00:12:04,784
Everything also works from the command line, on the same workspace.

103
00:12:05,239 --> 00:12:11,276
rdt info shows the link, the profile, and each MCU’s ELF check and memory map.

104
00:12:11,746 --> 00:12:16,460
symbols and ptype look up variables and their types, with no device needed.

105
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:27,096
read opens a structure to every field. With a tab name, it reads that tab’s items.

106
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:34,828
write changes one value, and confirms it by reading it back.

107
00:12:36,032 --> 00:12:38,485
dump shows memory as hex and ASCII.

108
00:12:40,214 --> 00:12:44,310
watch streams values, and can log them to a CSV file.

109
00:12:47,219 --> 00:12:54,131
send sends one SCPI command. With expect, the exit code says whether the reply matched.

110
00:12:54,609 --> 00:13:01,393
rdt console is the same console, in the terminal, with RDT’s own dot commands.

111
00:13:01,844 --> 00:13:05,470
add, list and tab edit a workspace without a device.

112
00:13:09,751 --> 00:13:15,810
And memory, watchlist and commands import and export parts of it, as in the GUI.

113
00:13:18,465 --> 00:13:19,937
That’s RDT.

114
00:13:20,237 --> 00:13:26,275
The user manual covers every feature in detail, with troubleshooting and where each file lives.

115
00:13:26,575 --> 00:13:30,713
Start with the simulator, then point RDT at your own board.

